Testing the first `ushort.MaxValue` `ulong`s of `ushort.MaxValue` Randoms on .NET 6 for collisions; which is a 32bit sample of a 64bit range; so shouldn't have collisions:
```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Runtime.InteropServices;

var values = new HashSet<ulong>();

for (int i = 0; i < ushort.MaxValue; i++)
{
    var rand = new Random();
    for (var c = 0; c < ushort.MaxValue; c++)
    {
        var value = 0ul;
        rand.NextBytes(MemoryMarshal.AsBytes(MemoryMarshal.CreateSpan(ref value, 1)));

        if (!values.Add(value))
        {
            Console.WriteLine($"Collision on Random {i} at value {c}");
        }
    }
}

Console.WriteLine("Done");
```
I run out of memory before getting a collision; so maybe it doesn't matter 😅

However it does entirely depend on the behaviour of `Interop.GetRandomBytes` as the seed; rather than being internally consistent (which the jump function should provide).
